# PeerJS signaling + ICE configuration.
#
# All values are OPTIONAL. With none set, the app falls back to PeerJS's free
# public infrastructure (0.peerjs.com broker + public STUN/TURN), which is
# rate-limited and often fails between two different networks. For a real
# deployment, point these at your own signaling host and TURN provider.
#
# These are NEXT_PUBLIC_* vars: they are inlined into the client bundle at
# build time. In CI, set them on the build step (see
# .github/workflows/deploy-pages.yml) via repo Variables/Secrets. Locally,
# copy this file to `.env.local`.
#
# WARNING: TURN credentials end up in the public JS bundle — browser WebRTC
# always exposes them. Use a provider that issues long-lived static
# credentials, and rotate them if leaked.

# --- Signaling server (self-hosted PeerServer). Omit to use 0.peerjs.com. ---
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PEER_HOST=peer.example.com
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PEER_PORT=443
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PEER_PATH=/
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PEER_SECURE=true
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PEER_KEY=peerjs

# --- ICE servers, option 1: runtime endpoint (preferred). ---
# URL fetched at connect time for fresh TURN credentials. Accepts either a
# bare array of ICE servers or { iceServers, ttl }. Two ways to use it:
#   a) Point directly at a CORS-open provider API, e.g. Metered:
#      https://<app>.metered.live/api/v1/turn/credentials?apiKey=<API_KEY>
#      (simplest; the apiKey is visible in the bundle — Metered's own pattern.)
#   b) Point at the proxy worker in ./worker to keep the API token secret.
# Takes precedence over the static TURN_URLS below; on fetch failure the
# client falls back to those, then to PeerJS public defaults.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ICE_SERVERS_URL=https://<app>.metered.live/api/v1/turn/credentials?apiKey=<API_KEY>

# --- ICE servers, option 2: static, build-time. Comma- or space-separated. ---
# STUN is enough for same-network / easy-NAT peers; TURN is required to relay
# when direct hole-punching fails (symmetric NAT, CGNAT, mobile, firewalls).
# Include a TURN entry on TCP/TLS port 443 for restrictive networks, e.g.
#   turns:turn.example.com:443?transport=tcp
# Only usable with providers that issue long-lived static credentials, which
# then sit in the public JS bundle.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STUN_URLS=stun:stun.l.google.com:19302
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURN_URLS=turn:turn.example.com:3478,turns:turn.example.com:443?transport=tcp
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURN_USERNAME=your-turn-username
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURN_CREDENTIAL=your-turn-credential
